pg('Jay A Career','Assignment 2 • Game Design Document\nFree realism upgrade / revised implementation')
p('Amr Hazem El Kotit\nGUC ID: 58-14399\nDMET909 • Video Game Design\nRevision: 13 October 2026')
image('Jay A Career - Revised World Map.png','The compact site connects preparation, delivery and rest without a larger town.')
h('Experience','A grounded, fictional desert laboratory and delivery game. Jay receives a briefing, handles one sealed package, negotiates with a customer, cleans the workspace and begins another day. The package is abstract: the game contains no real production procedure.')
h('Submission scope','This revision documents the authored JayACareer_Realism scene. Earlier Assignment 1 work and the previous Assignment 2 document remain preserved. The game, map, screenshots, evidence reports and this document should be reviewed together.')
pg('Design overview')
h('Player role and motivation','Jay is a junior worker in a small industrial site. The daily objective creates a readable order of tasks while negotiation introduces a modest financial choice. The player earns cash within the current session and learns the relationship between delivery, cleanup and rest by completing repeatable days.')
h('Design pillars','Believable scale: human proportions, enclosed rooms and manufactured furniture establish a consistent metre-based world. Readable work: preparation, storage and cleaning have separate locations. Reliable consequences: one package and one settlement belong to each day. Compact traversal: walking connects every task without driving or a large city.')
h('Art direction','The laboratory uses cool fixtures, painted cabinets, glass vessels and steel furniture. Exterior sand, worn asphalt, gravel and utility equipment support the desert setting. Warm bedroom light marks rest. Surface wear is restrained so interactions remain distinguishable from decoration.')
h('Requirements source','Assignment 2 (Assignment ).pdf, pages 1–3, is the requirements source. It asks for locations, at least 30 distinct objects, scale, boundaries, time, at least 20 mechanics, five action demonstrations, three Cinemachine behaviours and three lighting behaviours. Its stated submission time is October 18 at 4 PM, with no extensions; the PDF does not state a year.')
h('Reading guide','Pages 3–9 explain the world. Pages 10–15 catalogue 36 distinct objects. Pages 16–22 explain controls and 23 substantive mechanics, grouping opening/closing and offer alternatives together. Pages 23–30 connect the daily sequence, implementation evidence, validation, sources and reflection.')
pg('World map and connections')
image('Jay A Career - Revised World Map.png','Measured plan of the saved scene; X is east/west and Z is north/south.')
table(['Zone','Measured footprint','Connection'],[['Laboratory','12 × 12 m; X −13…−1, Z 5…17','South hinged entrance → access path'],['Road and exterior','44 × 27 m site; road 5 m wide','Access path → road crossing → customer apron'],['Customer area','12 × 4 m apron; X 4.5…16.5, Z 12.5…16.5','Counter faces approaching player'],['Bedroom','7 × 6 m; X 7…14, Z 5.5…11.5','Southwest entrance off exterior route']],[1.25,2.5,2.9])
p('The route is deliberately legible: leave the lab through its only entrance, cross the road, walk north beside the bedroom and approach the customer counter from the south. An unsuccessful deal reverses this route to the return shelf. Cleaning is inside the lab; sleep is across the road.')
pg('Location: laboratory')
h('Purpose and layout','The lab contains briefing, preparation, storage and cleaning in one enclosed 12 × 12 metre room. Dr. Hale stands near the centre-north aisle. Preparation is on the northwest bench; instruments occupy the northeast bench. Glassware and ventilation line the west work area. Storage and the return tray occupy the east side; cleaning is near the southeast entrance route.')
h('Terrain and materials','The dedicated concrete floor uses a tiled PBR surface with diffuse, normal and roughness-derived smoothness. Modular painted walls and windows enclose the room beneath roof modules and a continuous backing. The entrance has a real hinge pivot and a roughly 2.1 metre high opening. Benches sit at approximately 0.9 metre work height.')
h('Navigation and readability','Workstations are approached from the open aisle rather than through shelves or cabinets. The preparation package sits above its bench and the return tray projects in front of storage. Yellow handwritten notes identify tables, task stations and all four liftable props; liftable prompts show their names and controls. Context prompts appear when targeting a relevant collider within three metres. The labeled light switch is near the entrance.')
h('Consequences','Preparing activates visible stains and used glassware. These remain until the delivery outcome is settled and cleaning succeeds. Repeated preparation does not create another package. The lab therefore shows the current task state through both the objective text and the physical workspace.')
h('Environmental storytelling','Drawers, reagent bottles, test tubes, instrument housings, a fume hood, pipes, ventilation and a covered waste bin imply a working industrial room. Most of these are scenery. Their presence does not imply that the player can operate a real centrifuge, mix reagents or simulate laboratory chemistry.')
image('Evidence/lab-final.png','Laboratory work areas in the final authored scene.')
pg('Location: road and exterior')
h('Terrain','A five metre asphalt strip runs the full 27 metre north–south extent of the site. Yellow worn markings distinguish the roadway, and gravel shoulders connect it to sand. A short concrete access path links the laboratory entrance to the road. The environment remains a walking level: there are no cars, traffic simulation or driving controls.')
h('Infrastructure','Street lamps, a utility pole, electrical service box, air-conditioning unit, drums and fencing establish the site as a small serviced industrial property. The objects are placed around the route instead of across it. A warehouse facade gives the customer destination a plausible context without introducing another explorable building.')
h('Connections','From the laboratory entrance at approximately X −7, Z 5, the player walks south to the access path at Z 2.7, crosses the road near X 1 and proceeds north on its east side. The bedroom is reached from the southwest. The customer apron is reached farther north, with the counter directly ahead.')
h('Boundaries and feedback','Concrete side walls and north/south fences explain the limits visibly. Player bounds provide a second safeguard if the character reaches the edge. Sand, concrete and asphalt use different footstep sounds. This reinforces surface changes without changing movement speed or introducing friction puzzles.')
h('Lighting','A directional sun changes intensity, colour and angle on a 120 second cycle. This changes atmosphere without failing the objective or spawning hazards. The flashlight remains available outdoors and inside. Sleeping returns the ambient cycle to morning.')
image('Evidence/exterior-final.png','Exterior route, laboratory entrance and industrial boundaries.')
pg('Location: customer area')
h('Space and purpose','The customer stands behind a work-height counter on a 12 × 4 metre concrete apron. A warehouse facade, shutter seams, pallet, crate and safety sign make this a service and collection area. The counter is the physical handoff point, separating arrival with a carried package from a ready negotiation.')
h('Approach','The player approaches from the south at about X 9, Z 12.5. The counter sits near Z 14 and the customer near Z 15.4. The front approach leaves enough space to target the counter, place the package, then target the customer. The conversation uses a close-up camera rather than a persistent floating label.')
h('Usability','Only the correct daily package enables negotiation. Dropping while aiming at the counter docks it at the authored handoff slot. The customer will not begin an offer while the package is absent or still being carried. Ordinary crates and drums cannot substitute for the package.')
h('Decision and consequence','The customer offers $50. A accepts, C proposes $75 with a disclosed 50% acceptance chance, and R rejects. A choice resolves the negotiation once. A sale removes the package and increments cash once. Refusal or rejection pays nothing and leaves the same package available for return to the lab.')
h('Day variation','The same humanoid customer alternates between two clothing materials on subsequent days. This is a visual variant, not a new customer AI, a different negotiation model or a new economy. The offer rules stay consistent so the player can understand the daily loop.')
image('Evidence/customer-final.png','Customer and counter: the package must be present before the offer starts.')
pg('Location: bedroom')
h('Role','The bedroom is a separate 7 × 6 metre enclosed room east of the road. Its bed is the final station in the daily sequence. A desk, lamp and chair make the space read as a place to rest, while warmer lighting distinguishes it from the working laboratory.')
h('Route and scale','The entrance is on the southwest side. The player approaches at X 7.7, Z 4.4 and passes through the opening at Z 5.5. The desk is near the back wall at X 9.4, Z 10.6. The bed occupies the east side near X 12.7, Z 9.4. The centre of the room remains the walking approach to the bed.')
h('Bed interaction','O at the bed attempts sleep. Sleep is available only after settlement, any required return and cleaning. An early attempt displays the current objective instead of silently advancing the day. Successful sleep increments the day, hides the package, resets preparation and negotiation, resets daylight to morning and retains accumulated cash.')
h('Furniture interaction','O on the bedroom chair enters a bounded arrangement mode. WASD moves the chair, Q/R rotates it, Enter confirms a clear placement and Esc cancels to the starting transform. Movement stays inside the bedroom arrangement region. Confirmation refuses overlap with other colliders, preserving a usable space.')
h('Scope','The bed does not heal health, consume food, save to disk or advance an external calendar. The room provides task closure and a repeatable session reset. Quitting the game starts a fresh session next time; the document does not claim persistent progression.')
image('Evidence/bedroom-final.png','Bedroom route and final daily interaction.')
pg('Scale and boundaries')
table(['Element','Implemented scale / rule'],[['World','44 × 27 m; one Unity unit equals one metre'],['Laboratory','12 × 12 m; approximately 3.2 m wall height'],['Bedroom','7 × 6 m; approximately 3 m wall height'],['Road','5 m wide, with gravel shoulders'],['Door','Approximately 2 m wide panel and 2.1 m high opening'],['Work surfaces','Approximately 0.9 m above the room floor'],['Player collider','1.75 m capsule height, 0.28 m radius, 0.25 m step offset'],['Interaction range','3 m camera targeting range'],['Walking speed','5 m/s maximum; normalized diagonal input'],['Playable bounds','X −22…22; Z −3…24; fall recovery below Y −3']],[1.75,4.9])
h('Physical boundaries','Walls, roof pieces, cabinets and benches use colliders. The entrance panel rotates around its hinge and continues to collide when open. Closing is refused when its closed volume overlaps the player or a carryable object. Boundary walls and fences give the site an understandable edge.')
h('Walking clearances','The scene is arranged around an open laboratory aisle, a broad road crossing, a front counter approach and the bedroom centre. Decorative objects should stay outside these routes. Measurements are design dimensions from the authored transforms; a route check is still necessary because bounding colliders can occupy more space than a detailed mesh suggests.')
h('Recovery boundaries','Player clamping and fall recovery prevent leaving the playable site. The delivery package separately recovers at the preparation bench when it falls or leaves the bounds. Recovery preserves its daily identity, so the safety system cannot become a source of duplicate deliveries.')
pg('Time and world state')
h('Ambient time','The environmental cycle lasts 120 seconds. Sun colour shifts between warm daylight and cool low light; intensity and angle change continuously. The cycle is atmospheric, not a deadline. Walking slowly, rearranging the chair or inspecting the room does not fail the day.')
h('Task time','Task progression is an explicit ordered state sequence. Briefing unlocks preparation. Preparation produces one package. Docking at the counter permits negotiation. A successful sale moves to cleaning; an unsuccessful deal requires return first. Cleaning enables sleep. Sleep begins the next day. World lighting continues while tasks are active.')
h('Pause time','Esc opens the pause panel and freezes scaled time. Resume restores time and mouse capture. The same key first cancels chair placement when arrangement mode is active. Pausing during dialogue does not settle an offer or advance the day.')
h('Session persistence','Day and cash persist only within the running game. Starting a fresh game resets Day 1 and $100. Sleep retains money while resetting the package and briefing state. There is no disk save, autosave, campaign clock, resource expiry or real-time synchronization.')
h('Visual state','Workspace clutter is hidden before preparation, visible while work remains unresolved, and removed by successful cleaning. The package is hidden before preparation and after a sale or sleep. An unsold package remains available until it is returned. The customer material alternates by day, providing small visible variation.')
h('Why this structure','The ambient cycle makes the compact world feel active while explicit task states make progress dependable. The player can recognize both the passing atmosphere and the next action without being punished for learning the controls. Clear denial messages explain which dependency remains.')
objects=[
('Preparation bench','Bevelled worktop, metal legs, drawers and handles at work height. O creates the daily sealed package after briefing. One package and visible clutter appear; repeat preparation cannot duplicate it.'),
('Bedroom desk','Finished desk with a top, drawer and supporting frame. Static bedroom furniture supporting the lamp. It defines the personal space and keeps the bed approach readable. It does not open a computer interface or advance the daily loop.'),
('Cleaning sink','Cabinet bench with basin, tap and drain detail. C performs the cleanup action after settlement and required return. It removes stains and used glassware and enables sleep; early cleaning is refused.'),
('Wall cabinet','Raised storage cabinet with doors and handles. Static storage scenery above preparation. It explains supplies and creates vertical detail; cabinet doors are not separately operated, and their contents are not inventory.'),
('Steel shelves','Open industrial frame with multiple storage levels and worn surfaces. It holds cartons and frames return storage. The shelves establish storage context; their collider blocks walking through the frame. Shelf levels are not separate object types.'),
('Return tray','Blue receiving container with side handles, supported in front of storage. L while carrying the unsold package and aiming here docks the return. Only an unsuccessful resolved deal can use it; successful return enables cleaning.'),
('Centrifuge housing','Compact rounded instrument with lid and control detail. Static equipment on the northeast bench. It signals a professional laboratory; no real centrifugation process is simulated, and it does not modify or generate the package.'),
('Precision balance','Small instrument with a weighing platform and display housing. Static bench equipment. It adds a distinct measurement prop and scale reference; it does not weigh objects, consume reagents or change the delivery item.'),
('Lab terminal','Angled screen and instrument enclosure on the bench. Static visual equipment with a subtle screen material. It improves workstation readability; no computer interface, crafting menu or inventory opens when it is targeted.'),
('Fume hood','Tall ventilated enclosure with work surface and front opening. Static west-side laboratory station. It makes ventilation believable and establishes a specialist work area. Its collider contributes to navigation; no air-flow or chemistry system is operated.'),
('Test-tube rack','Narrow tubes supported by a structured rack. Static glassware display on the west worktop. It provides a recognizable laboratory object; tubes are not consumables and their repeated instances count as one catalogue type.'),
('Graduated beaker','Hollow cylindrical glass vessel with rim and base. Clean glassware is scenery; a separate used instance appears with preparation clutter. The used instance disappears on cleaning. Glassware itself does not produce the package.'),
('Conical flask','Hollow tapered vessel with a narrow neck and glass material. Static glassware on the west bench. Its distinct silhouette supports material and scale recognition; there is no mixing action or operational chemical recipe.'),
('Reagent bottle','Sealed bottle with cap and contrasting material. Static supply prop repeated as a group. Repetition suggests stored inventory without adding five distinct object types. Bottles cannot be consumed, sold or substituted for the delivery package.'),
('Lab stool','Round seat, legs and foot support at bench height. Static laboratory seating. It supports the working scale and occupies a small collider volume. Sitting is not implemented; the bedroom arrangement chair is a separate interaction using this model.'),
('Covered waste bin','Lidded container with a contrasting cap. Static near cleaning. It explains cleanup infrastructure and provides a recognizable disposal prop. It is not a separate disposal, scoring or inventory station; C belongs to the cleaning sink.'),
('Lab door','Painted industrial panel beneath a framed opening. O opens; Q attempts to close around a saved hinge pivot. It controls passage and retains collision. Closing refuses occupied space rather than trapping Jay or a carried prop.'),
('Window wall module','Framed industrial wall section with an opening. Static laboratory enclosure. It admits views and light while retaining wall collision. Windows do not open. Repeated window modules remain one object type regardless of their position.'),
('Solid wall module','Painted modular panel with structural framing. Static laboratory enclosure and navigation boundary. It prevents walking and targeting through the shell. Different instances and rotations count as one type; they do not imply destructible walls.'),
('Roof module','Flat metal roof piece with manufactured edges. Static overhead enclosure with continuous backing. It completes both camera views and keeps the lab spatially enclosed. Roof pieces are not climbable, removable or part of a construction system.'),
('Wall vent','Rectangular industrial ventilation grille high on the wall. Static service detail. It supports ventilation storytelling without an air-flow simulation. Its purpose is visual context, so it has no separate interaction prompt or progression consequence.'),
('Exhaust pipe','Narrow industrial pipe sections near the ceiling. Static utility detail. It connects the room visually to services; repeated pipe sections are one catalogue type. Pipes are not carryable and do not transport a simulated resource.'),
('Cable tray','Original overhead channel with restrained cable detail. Static near the back wall. It creates plausible electrical routing without a wiring puzzle. Its placement keeps cables outside the walking route and avoids turning decoration into an obstruction.'),
('Light switch','Small high-contrast wall plate near the entrance. O toggles four laboratory fixtures as one group. It changes local visibility independently of ambient time and flashlight. The switch does not reset task state or consume power.'),
('Ceiling fixture','Long rectangular light housing above work areas. Saved light sources respond to the wall switch. They provide cool room illumination. Fixture copies do not count as separate types; only one local lab light casts a shadow for cost control.'),
('Sealed delivery carton','Textured cardboard package with visible tamper strip. L picks up/places; R rotates; valid counter/return docks accept it. It represents the single daily transaction. Identity prevents substitution, duplicated preparation and additional payment after settlement.'),
('Supply crate','Small wooden industrial crate with manufactured edges. L carries it, R rotates it and L places it on a clear surface. It demonstrates ordinary prop handling without unlocking negotiation or paying cash. It cannot replace the daily package.'),
('Industrial drum','Worn blue plastic barrel with rim and lid detail. An empty laboratory variant is carryable; exterior copies are scenery. It adds storage realism. Ordinary drums cannot become packages, and colour or placement variants are not distinct types.'),
('Wooden pallet','Low timber platform beneath warehouse cargo. Static customer-area service prop. It supports cargo visually and distinguishes the receiving apron from the lab. It is not a vehicle, movable platform or separate storage inventory.'),
('Bedroom lamp','Small shaded lamp on a finished desk. Static bedroom decoration that makes the room read as a personal space. A separate saved warm room light provides illumination; this prop has no individual switch and does not duplicate the laboratory lighting mechanic.'),
('Warehouse facade','Industrial frontage with roller shutter and seams. Static background building at the customer area. It explains the destination and limits the view. The shutter does not open and its interior is outside playable scope.'),
('Chain-link fence','Industrial mesh boundary panels at site edges. Static visible boundary. It communicates the map limit before Jay reaches clamped bounds. Panels are not climbable or destructible; repeated panels count as one type.'),
('Street lamp','Tall roadside pole with lamp head. Static exterior infrastructure. It establishes road scale and a serviced site. No separate street-light switch is implemented. Lamp instances do not add new lighting behaviours to the three required demonstrations.'),
('Electrical service box','Metal utility enclosure mounted outside the lab. Static infrastructure. It suggests supplied power and adds believable exterior detail. There is no repair mechanic, fuse inventory or power outage objective associated with this prop.'),
('Air-conditioning unit','Vented exterior machine with fan housing. Static on the lab exterior. It supports the industrial setting; its equipment is not player-operated. Machinery ambience provides atmosphere without claiming a temperature or ventilation gameplay simulation.'),
('Bedroom bed','Finished mattress, frame, pillow and bedding. O attempts sleep once work is complete. It advances the day and resets morning while keeping session cash. Early sleep is denied, and it does not save progress to disk.')]
